    "created_at": "2026-04-28T01:34:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-28T01:34:46Z",
    "body": "We should probably define an algorithm for fetching a string which boils down to returning the first of:\n1. What the OS has cached.\n2. The string in the user's configured locale.\n3. The string in the en_US locale.\n\nSteps 2 and 3 are designed to mimic what the OS would normally do to populate the cache. The preference for the OS cache is because it'll be faster, less disruptive to the device, and be consistent with any OS UI surfaces which also refer to the device.\n\nRight now what Chromium does for step 2 is actually pick the first language ID returned by the device and only fall back to requesting English if the device doesn't respond to the request for a language list. It would be good to check what Linux and Windows do as well. I suspect Linux's logic is similar to Chromium's since the kernel doesn't (IIRC) have a locale setting while Windows might be cleverer.",
